<manifest xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android"
  package="dev.vpay.checkout_flutter">

  <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.INTERNET" />

  <!--
    `androidx.browser`'s own recommended manifest entry so `CustomTabsIntent`
    can resolve a Custom-Tabs-capable browser on API 30+'s package-visibility
    restrictions (`VpayCheckoutActivity.launchCustomTab`). No custom URL
    scheme entry here or anywhere in this plugin, on purpose — ADR-0021/D8.
  -->
  <queries>
    <intent>
      <action android:name="android.support.customtabs.action.CustomTabsService" />
    </intent>
  </queries>

  <application>
    <!--
      D5, revised 2026-09-16 ("browser, not WebView" — VpayCheckoutActivity.kt's
      own header has the full history): a plain Activity that launches the
      payer's browser as a partial (bottom sheet) Custom Tab and renders
      nothing of its own, non-negotiably `android:exported="false"`. This
      Activity takes a URL extra that carries a payer credential (the
      checkout session's own secret, in the URL fragment) — an exported
      Activity accepting that extra is a classic intent-redirection hole:
      any other app on the device could start it directly, handing it an
      attacker-chosen URL to load, or intercepting the extra by other
      means. No other app has any business starting this Activity, so it is
      launched only by this same app's own Flutter engine, with
      `startActivityForResult`.

      `Theme.Translucent.NoTitleBar` (a stock Android theme — this plugin no
      longer ships its own `styles.xml`; the old `Theme.Vpay.CheckoutSheet`
      existed only to host the WebView-based Material bottom sheet this
      revision removes) keeps the merchant's own Activity, one below this
      one in the same task, visible through the brief moments this Activity
      is on screen before the Custom Tab covers it and after it resumes.
    -->
    <activity
      android:name=".VpayCheckoutActivity"
      android:exported="false"
      android:theme="@android:style/Theme.Translucent.NoTitleBar"
      android:configChanges="orientation|screenSize|keyboardHidden" />

    <!--
      App Link intake. `VpayCheckoutActivity` above stays `exported="false"`
      — see its own comment and file header — and an `exported="false"`
      component cannot be the target of an external Intent, App Links
      included, at all. So this is a SECOND, exported entry point, kept as
      narrow as `VpayCheckoutAppLinkActivity.kt`'s own header can make it:
      it holds no state, never sees the checkout session's credential-
      bearing URL, and only forwards a public success/cancel URL to
      whichever `VpayCheckoutActivity` is currently open. That file's header
      has the full security tradeoff this widening buys and costs — stated
      loudly there and here rather than left implicit, per this change's own
      instructions.

      **This plugin deliberately declares NO `<intent-filter>` here**, and
      that is the whole point of the entry. `stopUrls` — the merchant's own
      `success_url`/`cancel_url` — are runtime configuration per checkout
      session (D2), so this shared plugin cannot know the host. The filter
      it would otherwise have to write, `<data android:scheme="https"/>`
      with no `android:host`, matches EVERY https URL on the device, and
      that is actively harmful rather than merely useless:

        - `minSdk` here is 21. On API 21-30 an unverified web
          intent-filter still joins the system "Open with" chooser, so
          every merchant app shipping this plugin would offer itself as a
          handler for every https link its payer ever taps, anywhere on
          the device. A payments SDK silently turning its host app into a
          candidate browser is not a tradeoff worth any deep-link
          convenience.
        - On API 31+ Android excludes unverified web links from that
          chooser, so the same filter routes nothing at all. Harmful on
          old Android, inert on new Android — there is no version where a
          hostless filter is the right answer.

      So the merchant declares the filter, for their own verified host, in
      their OWN app's manifest, targeting this activity by name:

        <activity android:name="dev.vpay.checkout_flutter.VpayCheckoutAppLinkActivity"
                  tools:node="merge">
          <intent-filter android:autoVerify="true">
            <action android:name="android.intent.action.VIEW" />
            <category android:name="android.intent.category.DEFAULT" />
            <category android:name="android.intent.category.BROWSABLE" />
            <data android:scheme="https" android:host="shop.example.com" />
          </intent-filter>
        </activity>

      …alongside serving a real `assetlinks.json` on that host. Until a
      merchant does both, no deep link reaches this activity and every
      checkout ends as `dismissed` — which D1/D4 already make
      correctness-complete (the poll decides, never the window).

      UNVERIFIED end to end: this repository has no HTTPS domain serving
      `assetlinks.json`, so no deep-link return has ever been driven
      through this activity. It is wired, not proven.
    -->
    <activity
      android:name=".VpayCheckoutAppLinkActivity"
      android:exported="true"
      android:noHistory="true"
      android:excludeFromRecents="true"
      android:theme="@android:style/Theme.Translucent.NoTitleBar" />
  </application>

</manifest>
